K to $140K)", "Unanimous expert ratings of Safe/Solid R", "Open seat removes incumbent-specific risk but no polling suggests a race"], "rationale": "The district's R+11 lean, a 7.9-point Republican national environment, and Climer's six-to-one fundraising edge point to a comfortable Republican win, and all nine expert raters call it Safe or Solid R. Markets price the Democrat at just 7 percent, consistent with a double-digit Republican margin.", "watch": "Any credible general-election polling showing Dittmer within single digits, or a major Climer scandal, would be the first sign of movement.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Ralph Norman (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Ralph Norman's retirement leaves an open seat in a district that leans Republican by about 11 points, and state Senator Wes Climer enters the general election as the heavy favorite. He has raised roughly $884,000 to Democrat Mallory Dittmer's $140,000, and national Republicans like Jim Jordan have headlined his fundraising. Dittmer has campaigned through town halls in Chester, Lancaster, and York County areas, but with no outside spending and little money, her path is narrow.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals, the favorable Republican environment, and expert consensus: every major rater rates the race Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give the Democrat about a 7 percent chance. A double-digit Republican win is the expected outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0458, "margin": -10.85}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0227, "margin": -14.5}]}